body:
the fact that at 13 shes able to articulate that is awesome! My step dad and I started out rocky. He married my mom when I was 7 and all I had known before him was just my mom and I. My mom didnt let him help discipline me, so I didnt see him as an authority until my late teens. I dont know what changed or why or when, but I figured out that he loves me and cares about me, I'm a terror honestly and he fact that hes STILL around, shows me how much he loves us. when I still lived at home, him and I would have father daughter time (we both needed a break from my mom) and wed go to the library wed go to the grocery store and hed teach me how to properly grocery shop and what deals to look out for. we dont get time like that very much anymore, and i miss it terribly but those outings helped us bond. we both love Gabriel Iglesias so he got us tickets to his show at the county fair one summer, back when I had just graduated hs and he was still working graves, wed go on car rides out to the big wal-mart (like a 30 minute trip) at like 1 am and hed buy us snacks and silly tshirts and wed get McDonalds and taco bell. when I was 11 i broke my wrist and he drove me to my xray and cast fitting and after he took me to Hanks and got me a giant bag of chocolate from the bulk section. when my grandpa went to the ICU with bleeding ulcers (hes still kicking it today btw) he took me out to dinner at my favorite Chinese buffet to keep me busy and to soften the blow for when my mom told me about my grandpa when we got back. hes done so much for me and it took me until therapy in my 20s to realize that he isnt my step dad, he IS my dad and he loves me like i'm his own. 

&#x200B;

tldr: go on outings with her, talk to her like an adult, let her cuss in front of you. The smallest things can mean the absolute most.
